--- body ---
## Author decision

Smelt must leave no Retired section in consolidated topic pages, in any
repository where the skill runs. The append-only journal is the place to
record retired rulings, grouped by retirement cause. This supersedes the
intermediate suggestion to retain retirements on topic pages for one round;
there is no Newly retired section either.

The author-rulings register is project-specific and does not follow the
ordinary topic-page template. Its format is a project-identifying title,
then active rulings grouped under useful subject headings. Entries are contextual jurisprudence: preserve the decision's substance,
relevant context and source; a later decision in a different context may differ.
Do not sacralize their wording or prefix entries with `pinned DATE`; dates
belong in the sources unless needed to understand a change. Qualifications sit beside the
ruling they qualify. Native-note citations link directly to their retained
source text or annex.

No introductory policy paragraph, generic Hazards/Open questions/Fallback
sections, Shared provenance inventory, or Stated intent and pending decisions
section is added to this register. Proposals and intentions remain in their
journal sources until the author makes a ruling. This is the format now
edited with the author in `memory/topics/commission.md` on PR #2263; preserve
its rulings rather than copying harness-specific decisions to other projects.

Integration and retirement of integrated rulings are primarily Brood's job.
Do not introduce an author-approval requirement for retiring a fully integrated
ruling, or use this format change to add authority boilerplate.

## Scope

Update the shared Smelt skill and its consolidation reference so subsequent
runs adopt this registry format and retirement behavior in their selected
project, including projects other
than the harness. Update affected size-accounting and editorial guidance as
needed, without rewriting existing journal entries or accepted reports.
Append retirement records to the selected project's journal, retaining the
ruling, its sources, reason and any verified integration destination.
Distinguish integration into rules/skills/documentation from retraction or
supersession; completed work and dated observations are history, not proof
that a ruling became a rule. Reuse existing source records rather than
re-appending the same retirement on every run.

Current application is in the unmerged seventh-smelt proposal PR #2263:
`memory/journal/2026/2026-11-08-smelt-retirement-causes.md`. Coordinate with
that proposal and the author's manual edits. This ticket changes the shared
skill, not other repositories' files from a harness session.

## Relevant files

- `skills/smelt/SKILL.md`
- `skills/smelt/references/consolidation.md` (page shape, caps and second pass)
- `scripts/memory_sizes.py` and affected checks, if their Retired exemption
  needs removal or adjustment to match the new page shape.
- Other active Smelt guidance found by a targeted reference audit.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Every selected project's author-rulings register uses the agreed compact
  format: project title, active rulings under useful subject headings, and
  entry-local context, scope and source, without `pinned DATE` prefixes. It is exempt from the ordinary topic
  section template.
- [ ] The register contains no introductory policy boilerplate, generic
  Hazards/Open questions/Fallback sections, Shared provenance inventory,
  pending proposals or unruled intentions. Useful qualifications and source
  links stay beside their rulings; historical sources remain intact.
- [ ] Adoption preserves each project's own rulings and author edits; it
  neither imports IDH rulings nor performs a cross-project write from the
  harness. A representative non-harness fixture demonstrates the format.
- [ ] Shared guidance identifies Brood as the primary owner of integration
  and retirement of integrated rulings, without adding an author approval
  gate or replacement boilerplate to the register.

- [ ] Shared Smelt instructions require no Retired or Newly retired topic
  section, for every selected project repository.
- [ ] Retired rulings are recorded in that project's append-only journal,
  grouped by cause, with sources and reasons; integration does not imply
  every ruling became a rule.
- [ ] Journal appends are explicitly authorized by the skill's write scope;
  existing journal entries and accepted reports remain unchanged.
- [ ] Page-shape, cap and second-pass guidance consistently reflects this
  behavior, with no remaining requirement for a Retired line or one-round
  Retired section.
- [ ] A representative consolidation check demonstrates topic removal and
  grouped journal retention without losing active rulings, overwriting author
  edits, or duplicating retirement records on a later run.
